# Prove an owner can still add a crew member, and that nobody can climb doing it.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE WORKFLOW, RUN ON DEMAND
# Self-signup is off, so "the owner adds someone" is the ONLY way anyone new ever
# gets into this app. If that path breaks, nobody new can ever get in again —
# which is worse than the permissive signup it replaced. It is also, by
# construction, an endpoint that creates an account at a chosen ROLE, i.e. the
# same shape as the privilege-escalation hole closed on profiles.role on
# 2026-07-29. Unit tests cover the rules as functions; only a live run covers
# them as an HTTP endpoint, which is where a hostile caller would knock.
#
# Not on every push, deliberately: it creates and deletes real auth users on the
# production project. Running it on every merge would be churn against live data
# for a path that only changes when someone edits it. Run it after touching the
# invite functions, the migration, or the role rules.
#
# It cleans up after itself and asserts the roster is byte-identical to what it
# found, so a half-finished run is loud rather than silent. See
# scripts/prove-crew-invites.py.

name: Verify crew invites

on:
  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  contents: read

jobs:
  invite-flow:
    name: An owner can add someone, and an installer cannot
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Prove the invite flow end to end
        env:
          SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN }}
          # Production. Named explicitly for the same reason scripts/pgq.sh
          # refuses to guess one: a run against the wrong project would report
          # success while proving nothing about the app the crew use.
          SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF: czprjcskmzzagdztqonm
        run: |
          if [ -z "$SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN" ]; then
            echo "::error::SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN is not set, so the invite flow was NOT verified."
            exit 1
          fi
          scripts/prove-crew-invites.py

  notify-failure:
    name: Tell Slack if nobody can be added any more
    needs: [invite-flow]
    if: failure()
    uses: ./.github/workflows/notify-failure.yml
    with:
      workflow: ${{ github.workflow }}
      cause: >-
        Adding a new crew member is broken, or the check that stops someone
        giving themselves more power than they have did not hold.
      detail: >-
        Nobody new can be given a login until this is fixed, and it may mean an
        invite could hand out the wrong level of access. The run log names the
        exact step that failed.
    secrets: inherit
